 ## Shared principles
 All ray functions operate on a sequence of samples taken along a ray intersecting the volume [G]. Each sample $i$ at position $t_i$ provides a scalar value $v_i$ (e.g., density or intensity) and potentially a color $c_i$ [G]. The functions aggregate these values to produce a single output per ray [G]. The family comprises four primary aggregation methods:
-1. **Maximum Value (MIP):** Returns the maximum scalar value encountered along the ray. It is forgiving of noise and provides intuitive structure but loses depth information [S2, book].
-2. **Average Value:** Returns the mean of scalar values along the ray [S1, unofficial].
